# Untitled

New Carbon Nitride \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{3}\) Additive for Improving Cationic Defects of Perovskite Solar Cells
Due to the loss of organic amine cations and lead ions in the structure of the iodine–lead methylamine perovskite solar cell, there are a large number of defects within the film and the recombination loss caused by grain boundaries, which seriously hinder the further improvement of power conversion efficiency and stability. Herein, a novel carbon nitride \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{3}\) incorporated into the perovskite precursor solution is a multifunctional strategy, which not only increases the light absorption strength, grain size, and hydrophobicity of the perovskite film, but also effectively passivates the bulk and interfacial defects of perovskite and verified by the first-principles density functional theory calculations. As a result, the efficiency and stability of perovskite solar cells are improved. The device with \(0.075\mathrm{~mg~mL^{-1}~C_{3}N_{3}}\) additive delivers a champion power conversion efficiency of \(19.91\%\) with suppressed hysteresis, which is significantly higher than the \(18.16\%\) of the control device. In addition, the open-circuit voltage of the modified device with the maximum addition as high as \(1.137\mathrm{~V~}\) is \(90.96\%\) of the Shockley–Queisser limit (1.25 V). Moreover, the power conversion efficiency of the modified device without encapsulation can maintain nearly \(90\%\) of its initial value after being stored at \(25~^{\circ}\subset\) and \(60\%\) relative humidity for \(500~\mathfrak{h}\) . This work provides a new idea for developing additives to improve the power conversion efficiency and stability of perovskite solar cells.
## 1. Introduction
In recent years, organic–inorganic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have attracted tremendous attention due to their excellent optoelectronic properties, such as high absorption coefficient, tunable band gap, small exciton binding energy, excellent ambipolar charge mobility, and long charge carrier diffusion lengths[1–3] Also, remarkable progress in power conversion efficiency (PCE) of the PSCs from \(3.81\%\) up to \(2.5.5\%^{[4,5]}\) has been achieved during the past decade. However, the long-term instability under environmental conditions and native defects of perovskite materials are the biggest challenge for the realistic implementation of perovskite-based devices.[6,7] Therefore, to find a more long-term stable and efficient PSCs, it is necessary to continue in-depth research on perovskite materials and other contents.
Previous researches indicated that defects of perovskite are mainly induced by the terminal halide ions or organic cations.[8–10] Organic cations are extremely unstable during the annealing process and are easily separated from
the perovskite lattice, so as to form grain boundary defects, which further generates uncoordinated \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) and/or Pb-I antisite defects.[11–14] A series of studies on new deposition technology, additive additions, doping, and interfacial modification, have improved the quality of perovskite film to obtain high-performance PSCs.[15–18]
Recently, a typical polymeric carbon nitrides (PCN), graphitic carbon nitride \(\left(\mathrm{g}{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\right)\) , has been extensively researched due to its advantages of inexpensive raw resources, good stability, and suitable band positions, which are beneficial for photovoltaic applications.[19–21] Jiang et al.,[22] Yang et al.,[23] and Liao et al.[24] improved the performance of the PSCs by introducing \(\mathrm{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) into the perovskite precursor solution to gear down the crystallization rate and passivate the surface defects of perovskite crystalline film. We recently also used \(\mathrm{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) to modify the dual interfaces of perovskite layer, which reduced the surface defects of the perovskite layer and increased the transportation and extraction of charge carriers.[25] However, only the narrow band of visible light \((<\!450\ \mathrm{nm})\) can be absorbed by pristine \(\mathrm{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) , on account of its wide bandgap \((2.83\,\mathrm{\eV})\) .[23,25] Therefore, the practical applications of pristine \(\mathrm{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) were greatly limited.
Besides \(\mathrm{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) , other PCNs have been developed, such as \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) . The structure of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) was predicted in 1997, and recently, it was
successfully synthesized by [Li.[26–28] Different from \(\mathrm{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) (Figure 1a), the structure of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) (Figure 1b) is composed of \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}{-}\mathrm{C}\) delocalized large \(\pi\) -conjugated bonds and its basic unit is triazine ring rather than heptazine ring in \(\mathrm{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) .[29] Comparing with \(\mathrm{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) \((2.82\ \mathrm{eV})\) , \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) has a higher visible light absorption intensity and a narrower band gap \((1.96~\mathrm{eV})\) (Figure 1c and Figure S1, Supporting Information). It is worth noting that the \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) additive has better xconductivity than \(\mathrm{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) . It is contributed to the low electronegativity carbon atoms (2.55) which are replaced by the high electronegativity \(_\mathrm{N}\) atoms (3.04) and restrict the free movement of electrons, thus resulting in the reduction of the conductivity of \(\mathrm{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) , but \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) does not.[28,29] Therefore, \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) can be better used in the field of photovoltaics.
In this work, \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) was produced by Ullmann reaction of cyanuric chloride on copper foil. After reaction, the product was washed by acid for following usage. A solvent additive engineering with \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) is carried out to modify the perovskite film. The champion solar cell has a PCE of \(19.91\%\) with a short-circuit current density \(\left(\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}\right)\) of \(22.43\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) , an open-circuit voltage \(\left(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\right)\) of \(1.12\,\mathrm{~V~}\) , and a fill factor (FF) of 0.78. The first-principles density functional theory (DFT) calculation was performed to investigate electronic properties of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) structure and interaction mechanism between \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) and perovskites. This work not only provides a new direction for the development and application of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) , but also opens up a new chemical additive for improving the efficiency and stability of PSCs.
## 2. Results and Discussions
## 2.1. Material Analysis of \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{3}\)
To detect the chemical state and elemental bonding of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) , the sample was subjected to X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis. As shown in Figure 1e, the C 1s spectrum can be divided into three peaks at 284.8 (carbon contaminants), and \(288.4~\mathrm{eV}\) \((\mathrm{N-C}=\mathrm{N}\) in \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3})\) . In Figure 1f, the N 1s XPS spectrum shows a sole peak at \(399.5~\mathrm{eV}\) \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{C}\) in \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3})\) . Although the sample had been washed with acid for many times, trace \(\mathrm{Cu}\) and Cl ions are still tightly adsorbed (Figure S3, Supporting Information). The atomic ratio of C to N is 1:1 after background correction, basically consistent with the theoretical value. The chemical structure of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) sample was further determined by Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectrum (Figure 1g). Specifically, the broad band in the region from 3100 to \(3500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) can correspond to the N-H band because of the protonation of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) . Peaks between 1300 and \(1700~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are mainly ascribed to the characteristic
vibration of \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}\) . The peak at \(809~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is the typical stretching vibration of triazine ring.[31]
In order to explore the effect of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) on performance of PSC, \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) was in the perovskite precursor solution to passivate the perovskite film, and n-i-p planar structure PSC was prepared (Figure 1h and Figure S4, Supporting Information).
## 2.2. \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{3}\) -Modified Perovskite Films
To advance the understanding of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) effects on photovoltaic properties of PSCs, studies of energy level and charge carrier transfer rates in perovskite film are crucial. The optical properties of perovskite thin films without and with \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) were evaluated through Ultraviolet-Visible absorption (UV-Vis) spectroscopy (Figure 2a). The absorption intensity of the \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) -modified perovskite films is stronger in visible light region than the control film, indicating the addition of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) contributes to enhance the light utilization rate of the perovskite film, which will improve the PCE of PSCs. Since the excessive addition reduces the light absorption, we consider the CN-7.5 (adding \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) with a concentration of \(0.075~\mathrm{mg~mL}^{-1}\) is defined as CN-7.5) perovskite film can be optimized for following research. Compared with the control perovskite \((1.59~\mathrm{eV})\), the CN-7.5 perovskite \((1.61~\mathrm{eV})\) shows wider band gaps, which helps to increase \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of PSC devices (Figure 2b). The work function decreases by \(0.17~\mathrm{eV}\) from 4.76 to \(4.59~\mathrm{eV}\) and the highest occupied state increases by \(0.2~\mathrm{eV}\) from 1.03 to \(1.23~\mathrm{eV}\) (Figure 2c). To visualize the change of energy level on the perovskite before and after \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) modification, the schematics of planar PSCs are displayed in Figure 2d. Considering that perovskite:CN-7.5
possesses lower conduction band energy level than the control can effectively reduce the Schottky barrier between perovskite and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) electron transport layer (ETL), thereby boosting the electron extraction and suppressing back-transfer and recombination.[32]
In the steady-state photoluminescence (PL) spectra (Figure 2e), the emission peaks are approximately at 776 and \(771\ \mathrm{nm}\) for the control and CN-7.5 film, respectively. And the PL intensity of CN-7.5 sample is obviously weaker than the control sample, indicating an enhanced charge extraction and suppressed carrier recombination, which can result from the passivation effect.[33] Comparing with the pristine perovskite, both the fast decay time \(\left(\tau_{1}\right)\) and the long decay time \(\left(\tau_{2}\right)\) in the \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\)-added perovskite film are decreased, revealed by time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectra. Moreover, the shorter carrier lifetime \(\left(\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}\right)\) is 133.48 and 60.53 ns for the perovskite film without and with \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) additive, respectively (Figure 2f and Table S1, Supporting Information). These results indicate that the incorporation of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) contributes to reduce the non-radiative recombination centers as well as accelerate efficient charge carrier extraction and transport to improve the efficiency.
The XRD patterns of the control and CN-7.5 modified perovskite film evaluated the effect of the \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) on perovskite crystallinity (Figure 3a). Almost no offset of main characteristic XRD peaks for (110) and (220) planes of perovskite is observed in \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\)-modified perovskite films, suggesting that the additive does not participate in constructing the perovskite framework.[34] Furthermore, the diffraction peak of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) does not appear in CN-7.5 film, reveals \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) can enhance the stability of PSCs by suppressing the production of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\).[35] In order to conduct qualitative analysis of perovskite films in the decomposition process, XRD analysis was performed on perovskite films with and without \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) stored in an environment with relative humidity (RH) of \(60\%\) for 7 days at room temperature. By comparing XRD peaks of the control and CN-7.5 film under the same condition, the diffraction intensity
of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) in the control film is much stronger than CN-7.5 film (Figure S5, Supporting Information). It proves that the addition of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) can improve the water resistance of the perovskite film.
The effect of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) addition on perovskite crystallization was further characterized by SEM. Compared with the control film, the \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\)-modified film has smoother surface, larger grains, and less grain boundaries (Figure 3b-c). The average grain size was increased from 13.8 to \(342\ \mathrm{nm}\), and the pinhole numbers were reduced after \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) modification (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Such, high-quality perovskite films facilitate the charge transfer and charge collection.[36,37] Comparing XPS of Pb 4f, all peaks shift to lower binding energies in the perovskite film after addition of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) (Figure 3d), indicating that the lone electrons pair on \(_\mathrm{N}\) of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) are coordinated with \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) ions of perovskite to passivate the positively charge defects, so as to increase the stability of perovskite films.
To further verify the \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\)-modified perovskite film by transmission electron microscopy (Figure S7, Supporting Information), the large amount of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) at the grain boundaries of continuous perovskite grains maintains a good crystal structure, which directly proves that the \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) additive can bridge the plate perovskite particles. Furthermore, the FT-IR spectroscopy of control and \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\)-modified perovskite film was depicted to evaluate the effect of the \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) on perovskite (Figure S8, Supporting Information). Besides the characteristic peaks corresponding to pristine perovskite, a new peak appeared at \(1642\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in the \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\)-modified perovskite films, which was caused by the red shift of the \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}\) bond of the \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) (the \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}\) bond bending vibration at \(1631\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for the \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) in Figure 1g). This demonstrates the chemical interaction between \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) additive and perovskite.
Moreover, contact angles of the control and CN-7.5 sample are increased from 32.75 to \(59.5^{\circ}\) after addition of \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) (Figure 3e-f), suggesting that the hydrophobicity of the CN-7.5 sample is higher than that of the control film. The modified perovskite film equally exhibited
stronger hydrophobicity with the continuously augment the amounts of \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) (Figure S9, Supporting Information). In addition, Figure S9, Supporting Information shows droplets containing \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) diffuse more slowly than those in the control film. It is obvious that the amount of absorbed moisture from the environment was significantly decreased after the addition of \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \).[38,39] The improved moisture stability may be attributed to \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) interaction with uncoordinated \( \mathrm{Pb}^{2+} \) or \( \mathrm{MA}^{+} \)to limit cation migration, passivity, the defect and reduce the degradation active site, as discussed in the next section.
## 2.3. Mechanistic Research of \( \mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{3} \) Modulation
The DFT calculation is applied to explain the interaction mechanism between perovskite and \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \).[40,41] A simple structural model was established to understand the electron distribution of \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \), and the \( _\mathrm{N} \) atom on the \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) skeleton presents strong red regions, which is caused by the lone electrons pair of \( _\mathrm{N} \) atoms (Figure S11, Supporting Information). Bond lengths between \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) and \( \mathrm{MA}^{+} \)or \( \mathrm{Pb}^{2+} \) of perovskite were calculated by optimizing the model structure (Figure S12, Supporting Information), in which bond length of \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) - \( \mathrm{MA}^{+} \)is \( 1.887\,\mathrm{~\AA~} \) (belonging to a hydrogen bond). Additionally, it can be determined that complexes of \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) - \( \mathrm{MA}^{+} \)and \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) - \( \mathrm{Pb}^{2+} \) are effortless to form by calculating the \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) complexation energy with uncoordinated \( \mathrm{MA}^{+} \)and \( \mathrm{Pb}^{2+} \) cations (Table S2, Supporting Information). These studies show that undercoordinated \( \mathrm{MA}^{+} \)and \( \mathrm{Pb}^{2+} \) cationic defects could be passivated by \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) complexation.
To confirm the charge density difference of interfaces between \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) and perovskites, the simulations of perovskite layer with \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) are shown for both MAI and \( \mathrm{PbI}_{2} \) -terminated slabs of perovskite. There are obvious electron accumulations between the \( _\mathrm{H} \) atoms at the MAI-terminated and the \( _\mathrm{N} \) atoms of \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \), which can be attributed to the formation of strong hydrogen bonds (Figure 4a). The transfer of electrons between \( \mathrm{Pb} \) atoms of the \( \mathrm{PbI}_{2} \) - terminated and \( _\mathrm{N} \) atoms of \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) (Figure 4b) not only provides visualization of the strong coordination bonds between \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) and \( \mathrm{PbI}_{2} \), but also indirectly shows that \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) acts as a bridge between grains of the perovskite film.[42] In order to further combine the actual experiment, we performed a series of simulations of defect structures (Figures S13 and S14, Supporting Information), and calculated its density of states (DOS). In the MAI-terminated module, DOS of the iodine vacancy defects did not change significantly before and after the addition of \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \), which proves that it cannot passivate iodine vacancy defects (Figure 4c).
## 2.4. Photovoltaic Performance of PSCs
2.4. Photovoltaic Performance of PSCsThe performance of the PSC devices is summarized, showing the influence of the addition of \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) with different concentrations (Figure 5a and Table S3, Supporting Information). When the concentration of \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) is \( 0.075~\mathrm{mg~mL}^{-1} \), the photovoltaic performance increased remarkably and reached the maximum PCE (19.91%), \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) of \( 22.43~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2} \), \( V_{\mathrm{OC}} \) of 1.17 V, and FF of 0.78. It shows the statistical distribution of the PCE of 10 data points in each different concentration of \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \)-treated cells (Figure 5b). Obviously, the PCE of the CN-7.5 device increased by \( 17.95\,\pm\,0.21\% \) up to \( 19.71\,\pm\,0.24\% \), in accordance with UV-vis absorption spectra (Figure 2a). To crosscheck the short-circuit current density, we determined the external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra, along with the integrated current density in Figure 5c. An impressive external quantum efficiency exceeding \( 80\% \) in the wavelength region from 400 to \( 750\ \mathrm{nm} \) was obtained. The integrated current density from EQE almost coincided with the current density–voltage (J-V) curves. Moreover, the forward and reverse J-V curves of the control and CN-7.5 device reveal that the addition of \( C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3} \) significantly reduces the hysteresis of the perovskite films, which is due to the effective passivation of the surface and bulk defects of the perovskite films (Figure S15 and Table S4, Supporting Information).As can be seen from Figure 5d, the value of trap-filled limit \( (V_{\mathrm{TFL}}) \) of the control and CN-7.5 perovskite film is 0.362 and \( 0.323\mathrm{~V~} \), respectively. Consequently, calculation analysis indicated that the value of the charge trap-state density \( \left(\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{trap}}\right) \) of CN-7.5 perovskite film is
